  "revised_answer": "The photoelectric effect occurs when light shines on a metal surface and causes the emission of electrons. According to Einstein's photoelectric equation, the maximum kinetic energy of an emitted electron is $K_{\\text{max}} = h\\nu - \\Phi$. In this equation, $h\\nu$ represents the energy of the incident photon and $\\Phi$ is the work function, which is the minimum energy required to remove an electron from the metal. The stopping potential $V_0$ is the reverse potential required to stop the most energetic electrons. It is related to the maximum kinetic energy by the relation $K_{\\text{max}} = e V_0$. By substituting this into the photoelectric equation, we get $e V_0 = h\\nu - \\Phi$. Solving for the stopping potential gives $V_0 = \\frac{h}{e}\\nu - \\frac{\\Phi}{e}$. This shows that the stopping potential $V_0$ increases linearly with the frequency $\\nu$ of the incident light, provided the frequency is above the threshold frequency $\\nu_0$. Below this threshold, no electrons are emitted. **Quick check:** If you repeat this experiment with a different metal (one with a higher work function), how would the slope of the $V_0$ vs. $\\nu$ graph change?"
}
